# Seal mode and House mode: how it works

One sentence: **seal says what the patient needs. House mode answers it with a
differential: the fellows, who run on Opus, each break the need into sub-goals
from their own stance, a deterministic harness merges and validates their
charts, residents (Sonnet) and nurses (Haiku) do the work under a resource
governor, and every claim is bound to a test that was RED first.**

## Vocabulary

| Word | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Patient | the project (a repo) |
| Need | one goal G0 with acceptance criteria: "the patient requires X to be complete" |
| Fellow | Foreman, Chase or Cameron: an Opus agent that charts the Need from its own stance |
| Chart | a fellow's tree of sub-goals, then sub-goals of those, and so on to leaves |
| Merged chart | the one goal tree the harness builds from the three charts |
| Leaf | a goal small enough for one agent to finish and one test to prove |
| Resident | a Sonnet agent that reaches a leaf needing judgment or code |
| Nurse | a Haiku agent that does a mechanical leaf; the army is many nurses in one batch |
| Harness | the Rust code (`seal-house`): validates, merges, schedules, throttles. Deterministic, no opinions |
| House | the Claude session acting as harness engineer: it dispatches what the harness emits and reports back |

## The two modes

```
 SEAL MODE (state the need)                 HOUSE MODE (the differential)
 --------------------------                 -----------------------------
 patient needs X                            RAG context pack (+ Wilson's facts)
   |                                           |
   v                                           v
 Need G0 in seal db  --------------->   FOREMAN     CHASE        CAMERON      (Opus, parallel)
 (memory: db + RAG)                     deeper tree  failure      alternative
                                                     modes        routes
                                              \         |         /
                                               harness MERGE + VALIDATE
                                                       |
                                          merged goal tree in seal db
                                                       |
                                   harness batch --> House dispatches
                                          RESIDENTS (Sonnet)   NURSES (Haiku)
                                                       |
                                    report + RED-first test, Inspector audit
                                                       |
                                     G0 reached --> prognosis --> seal db + RAG
```

**Seal mode** is small on purpose. It records the Need and serves memory (the db
and the RAG). It does not chart.

**House mode** is where all breaking-down happens, and it is done by the
fellows, not by one planner. The differential is the chart: three stances on the
same Need, reconciled, never averaged.

## Who does what (hard rules)

| Tier | Agents | Does | Never does |
|---|---|---|---|
| Opus | Foreman, Chase, Cameron, Wilson, Inspector, Dr House | fellows chart; Wilson states verified facts; Inspector audits the log; Dr House makes one leap; House reconciles | executes a leaf |
| Sonnet | residents | leaves that need judgment or write code | decomposes (returns `blocked: too big` instead) |
| Haiku | nurses | mechanical leaves: read, grep, run a named test, collect, format | writes code, decides anything |

Only the House session spawns agents. No agent spawns another (nesting is
unverified in Claude Code, so nothing depends on it).

## The three charts (stance, not domain)

Each fellow gets the same Need, the same RAG context pack and the same schema.
They differ only in what they are asked to produce.

| Fellow | Chart is | Every node must carry | Board shape |
|---|---|---|---|
| Foreman | the structure: root cause first, then the parts under it, as deep as it goes | `parent` | tree, "goes deeper" |
| Chase | the failure modes: what can break under each goal, as guard goals that become tests | `guards` (the goal it protects) | ladder, "what breaks" |
| Cameron | alternative routes to the same goal, each with its cost | `alt_of` (the goal it competes with) | lanes, "another way" |

Wilson is not a chart. He states verified facts (file exists, test exists, test
is currently red) with a source. The validator also checks path and test
existence in code, so Wilson is only needed for disputed facts. Inspector audits
the log and never charts.

## The merge (deterministic, in code)

1. **Backbone** is Foreman's tree. Every Foreman goal is kept.
2. **Guards.** Each Chase node becomes a leaf test-goal under the goal named in
   `guards`. A Chase node that guards nothing is rejected.
3. **Alternatives.** Each Cameron node joins an ALT group with the goal named in
   `alt_of`. The scheduler runs exactly one member per group: the lowest
   estimated cost among members not refuted by a fact. The others are retired
   with the recorded reason `cost` (a `reroute` or `reprice` route, so the
   losing route is a recorded replacement, never silently dropped).
4. **Refutation.** A Wilson fact with `refutes: <goal id>` kills that goal and
   its subtree. The fellow who proposed it must re-chart that subtree and
   supply a replacement route, at most twice.
5. **Leap.** If a subtree is still dead after two re-charts and the patient
   still needs it, and the cause is missing innovation, Dr House gets one
   five-minute leap. It re-enters as a hypothesis and needs a RED test.
6. **Conflicts the harness cannot resolve** (two fellows write the same file
   with no ordering; two different tests claim the same name) go back to the
   fellows once, then to the user as Open.

## The loop, step by step

1. **Need.** `house_need(project, goal, acceptance[])` creates G0 in the seal db.
2. **Context.** `rag_search` builds the context pack: compressed memories of the
   relevant files, symbols, tests and past decisions, capped at a token budget.
3. **Chart.** House dispatches Foreman, Chase and Cameron in one message (plus
   Wilson if his gate passes). Each calls `house_chart(fellow, chart)`.
4. **Merge and validate.** `house_merge()` applies the merge rules and the
   validator. Violations come back by fellow; that fellow re-charts only its own
   part. Three rejections of the same chart escalate to the user.
5. **Schedule.** `house_next` returns the next batch: leaves whose `needs` are
   reached, that fit the governor's slots, with no file conflicts. Each entry has
   the model, the prompt pack, the test to run and the resource limits.
6. **Dispatch.** House sends exactly that batch in one message.
7. **Report.** `house_report(goal, status, test, evidence)`. `reached` is
   accepted only if the bound test's first recorded run was RED. Otherwise it
   stays `active` and the board flags it (`goal_unproven`).
8. **Audit.** The Inspector re-runs claimed greens before the prognosis. A
   struck claim leaves the verdict.
9. **Unlock.** When all children of a parent are reached, the parent's integration
   test runs and the parent resolves. Repeat from 5.
10. **Close.** G0 reached: the prognosis is written to the seal db and the
    decisions are indexed into the RAG, so the next chart starts smarter.

## Chart schema

```json
{ "need": "G0", "fellow": "Foreman",
  "goals": [
    {"id":"G1","parent":"G0","text":"...","leaf":false},
    {"id":"G1.1","parent":"G1","text":"...","leaf":true,"tier":"sonnet","class":"code",
     "needs":[],"writes":["crates/x/src/a.rs"],"reads":["crates/x/src/b.rs"],
     "test":{"name":"a_rejects_empty","cmd":"cargo test -p x a_rejects_empty","class":"test_unit"},
     "est":{"ram_mb":800,"secs":90,"tokens":30000}} ] }
```

Chase nodes add `"guards":"G1.1"`. Cameron nodes add `"alt_of":"G1.1"`.

## Validator rules (each is a unit test in `seal-house`)

1. Parent links form a tree and `needs` forms a DAG. No cycles.
2. Depth at most 6. Fan-out at most 7 per node.
3. A non-leaf has at least 2 children. One child is padding.
4. Every leaf has a tier, a test with a command, and an estimate.
5. A Haiku leaf has empty `writes` and a class in `read | grep | run_test | collect | format`.
6. A leaf's `est.tokens` is within the tier cap (Sonnet 40,000; Haiku 8,000). Over the cap means decompose further.
7. Two leaves that write the same file are ordered by `needs`.
8. Every leaf's test is distinct and nameable. A whole-workspace test is not a leaf test.
9. Every `reads` and `writes` path exists, or the leaf declares `creates`.
10. Chase nodes name a goal in `guards`; Cameron nodes name one in `alt_of`.

## Resource governor (how the computer is not throttled)

It runs on every `house_next`, never once at start. It reads free RAM, logical
cores and load, and it never trusts a fixed number.

| Job class | Declared cost | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `llm` (agent call) | about 0 local RAM | bounded by the 20 concurrent subagent cap |
| `read` / `grep` / `collect` | 50 MB | |
| `test_unit` (`cargo test -p`, `pytest -k`) | 800 MB | |
| `build` (`cargo build`, `next build`) | 1,500 to 2,000 MB | |
| `bench` | exclusive | nothing else runs |

Slots for a class = `min(class cap, floor((free_ram - reserve) / cost))`, where
`reserve = max(2 GB, 15% of total RAM)`. Heavy classes (`test_unit`, `build`)
share a global cap of 6. If free RAM falls under the reserve, heavy slots drop
to 0 and only `llm` and light leaves are scheduled until it recovers. The
measured peak RSS of each finished job updates its class cost (moving average,
stored as `CostObs` nodes). Every shell command in a batch is wrapped with
`CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2` and below-normal priority.

## Smart testing (the suite does not run when it does not have to)

Stored in the seal db: `(Test)-[:COVERS]->(File)` and `TestRun{key,status,secs,ram_mb}`.

- **Selection.** A leaf touching files F runs the tests that cover F plus its
  declared test, most-recently-failed first, then cheapest first, stopping at the
  first failure.
- **Result cache.** `key = hash(test command + content hash of covered files + lockfile hash)`.
  A cached GREEN is reused. A cached RED is never reused.
- **Three levels.** L0 per leaf: affected tests only. L1 per parent: its
  integration test when its children are done. L2 the full suite: only at the G0
  gate, and only when you ask or in CI.
- **Flake guard.** A test that changes result with no file change is quarantined
  and reported, never counted as proof.
- **RED first.** A goal's bound test must have been RED on its first recorded run.

## Seal RAG (CLaRa-inspired, in Rust)

CLaRa (Apple, arXiv 2511.18659) compresses documents into continuous vectors (up
to 16x), retrieves and reranks in that compressed space, and trains retrieval and
generation together through a differentiable top-k. `seal-rag` keeps the shape of
that idea and drops what needs a trained model:

| CLaRa | seal-rag | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Learned compressor to continuous memory tokens | Extractive compressor to about 1/16 of the chunk (signatures, doc comments, salient lines) plus a 256-dim hashed n-gram embedding behind an `Embedder` trait | no GPU, no model download, deterministic |
| Retrieve and rerank in the compressed space | Hybrid BM25 plus vector search over the compressed memory, fused by reciprocal rank | same idea, trainless |
| Generator reads compressed memories | Fellows and residents read compressed memories; `rag_expand(chunk)` returns full text on request | the 16x saving is kept |
| Joint training through differentiable top-k | A utility prior: chunks cited in a reached goal gain score, chunks retrieved but unused lose it | a bandit-style stand-in, not gradient training |
| SCP supervision (QA and paraphrase) | none | out of scope |
| Graph | 1 to 2 hop expansion over the seal db (callers, tests, decisions) | uses the db we already have |

**Not claimed:** continuous latent reasoning, a differentiable top-k, parity with
CLaRa's benchmarks. The crate reports its own measured recall@k and compression
ratio on a fixture, against a full-text BM25 baseline.

## Seal db schema for this

- Nodes: `Project`, `Goal{id,text,leaf,tier,status,class,by}`, `Test{name,cmd,class}`,
  `File{path,hash}`, `Chunk{id,file,span,mem,vec,utility}`, `TestRun{key,status,secs,ram_mb}`,
  `CostObs{class,ram_mb}`, `Decision{text}`, `AltGroup{id}`
- Edges: `DECOMPOSES_INTO`, `NEEDS`, `TOUCHES`, `COVERS`, `BOUND_BY`, `GUARDS`,
  `ALT_OF`, `RETIRED{reason}`, `RETRIEVED_FOR`, `USED_BY`, `IN_FILE`, `NEXT`

## Tools (added to `seal-mcp`)

`house_need`, `house_chart(fellow, chart)`, `house_merge`, `house_next`,
`house_report`, `house_status`, `rag_index`, `rag_search`, `rag_expand`. Every
call appends the matching event (`chart`, `goal`, `test`, `dispatch`, `done`) to
`house-events.jsonl`, so the board shows each fellow's chart live. Each chart on
the board is that fellow's own output, drawn in the shape of their stance.

## Board: heartbeats and easter eggs

Each character's chart has its own heartbeat and an ASCII-art easter egg
registry. This is the part that makes the room feel alive; it never carries
data.

**Signature heartbeats** (the ECG under each name, amplitude still tracks the
nurse count):

| Character | Waveform |
|---|---|
| Foreman | a sharp, tall QRS with an extra spike: ambition |
| Chase | a slow, even metronome: nothing surprises him |
| Cameron | rounded, soft peaks |
| Wilson | small, perfectly regular pulses |
| Inspector | square pulses, a scanline ticking down a checklist |
| Dr House | an arrhythmia: irregular, then a single enormous spike on the leap, then flat |

**Easter eggs** are ASCII art drawn in ink grey, never in data colours, in a
corner of the card or as a faint watermark. A seeded RNG (seed = run id plus
agent plus event index) picks them, so a run replays identically. Triggers:

- a theory marked dead: a crossed-out `LUPUS` tag, sometimes with `(it's never lupus)`
- a leap fired: a small cane tapping
- a goal reached with a RED-first test: a tiny mortarboard or a check
- all gates clear: `EVERYBODY LIES. THE TESTS DON'T.`
- a struck claim: a tiny clipboard with a red tick
- a rare, random 1-in-40 egg on any event: a pill bottle, a whiteboard marker,
  a stethoscope, a piano

Rules: eggs never overlap a chart node or a gate row, `prefers-reduced-motion`
turns the animation off but keeps the art, and `?eggs=0` hides them. The egg
registry is a plain array of strings in `board.html`, so adding one is one line.
A test asserts the seeded pick is deterministic and that no egg is chosen
outside its trigger set.

## Worked example (numbers are illustrative `[est]`)

Need: "seal supports a vector index". Foreman charts 3 sub-goals deep to 9
leaves. Chase adds 4 guard goals (empty index, dimension mismatch, crash during
persist, stale entries after delete). Cameron adds one alternative to the HNSW
leaf: brute-force flat scan, cheaper. Wilson reports `hnsw.rs` does not exist
and the index tests are green today. The merge keeps one member of the ALT
group by cost, retires the other with a reason, and the harness validates
13 leaves. Free RAM is 6 GB, so the reserve is 2.4 GB and `test_unit` slots are
`floor(3.6 GB / 0.8 GB) = 4`.

- Batch 1: 2 nurses (index the repo; run baseline tests) and the 2 residents
  whose leaves have no `needs`.
- Batch 2: three leaves unlock. Two write different files and run together; the
  third writes a file batch 1 wrote, so it waits.
- Mid-run free RAM drops to 2 GB: `house_next` returns only light leaves until it
  recovers. Nothing is killed.

## Limits

The harness cannot spawn Claude agents itself; House dispatches the batches it
emits. Estimates are priors until the `CostObs` average has data. RAG recall is
measured on a fixture, not on your whole disk. The validator checks that a chart
is well-formed, not that it is a good plan; plan quality is the fellows' job and
the gates catch it only through the tests. The merge treats a Cameron
alternative as cheaper only by its declared estimate, which can be wrong.
